  "title": "Chapter 17 – Ohm’s Law and Electrical Measurements",
  "order": 15,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "ap2btzm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Ohm's Law establishes that potential difference (V) is directly proportional to electric current (I) at constant temperature.",
        "Resistance (R) measures opposition to current flow and depends on conductor length, area, material resistivity, and temperature.",
        "Ammeters are low-resistance instruments connected in series to measure circuit current.",
        "Voltmeters are high-resistance instruments connected in parallel to measure potential difference across components.",
        "Electrical power consumed in a circuit is given by P = VI = I²R = V²/R and is measured in Watts."
      ],
      "id": "navdn2x"
    },
    {
